E3	openrouter	\N	v1.0	GENERAL	{"answer": "• **Fair Trade Practices**: Listed entities must disclose monetary fines, penalties, and non-monetary sanctions paid regarding anti-competitive conduct or unfair trade practices to substantiate market integrity.\\n• **Related Party Exposure**: BRSR Core mandates reporting the concentration and percentage of total purchases, sales, or financial transactions executed with related parties to ensure transparent governance.\\n• **Leadership Diversity**: Organizations must report the structural composition of the Board of Directors and Key Managerial Personnel (KMPs), specifically detailing gender representation and independent directorships.\\n• **Ethics and Compliance Training**: Mandatory metrics track the coverage percentage of governing body members, key executives, and employees trained on anti-bribery, anti-corruption, and code of conduct policies.\\n\\n> ⚠️ **Caution**: This guidance is generated using general AI regulatory knowledge (no direct statutory chunk was matched in the local knowledge base). AI may make mistakes; evaluate carefully and verify against official SEBI / corporate filings.", "run_id": "", "grounded": false, "citations": [], "is_fallback": false, "indicator_code": "GENERAL"}	1.0000	\N	22374	proposed	\N	\N	\N	151c969e-8a80-4c66-a4ae-99d79aa4c112	2026-10-10 11:19:00.427009+05:30	2026-10-10 11:19:00.427035+05:30
E3	openrouter	\N	v1.0	GENERAL	{"answer": "• **Regulatory Purpose**: Renewable energy certificates (RECs) are accounted for to demonstrate compliance with the SEBI LODR Regulation 34(2)(f) and to enhance transparency in the company's environmental, social, and governance (ESG) reporting.\\n• **Scope & Boundary**: RECs are accounted for within the BRSR Core framework, which covers the company's renewable energy consumption and production. The scope includes all RECs issued or acquired by the company during the reporting period.\\n• **Calculation Rules**: The calculation of RECs is based on the GHG Protocol and GRI Standards, which provide a standardized framework for reporting greenhouse gas emissions and renewable energy consumption. The company must disclose the total amount of RECs issued or acquired, as well as the amount of RECs retired or cancelled.\\n• **Audit Verification**: The company's RECs accounting must be audited and verified by an independent auditor to ensure compliance with the SEBI LODR Regulation 34(2)(f) and the BRSR Core framework.\\n• **Disclosure Requirements**: The company must disclose the RECs accounting information in the BRSR report, which must be filed with the relevant regulatory authorities.", "run_id": "ESG-2023-001", "grounded": true, "citations": [{"doc": "BRSR Guidance Note", "text": "SEBI LODR Regulation 34(2)(f)", "score": null, "section": "Regulation 34(2)(f)", "version": "BRSR-2023"}], "is_fallback": false, "indicator_code": "GENERAL"}	1.0000	\N	10735	proposed	\N	\N	\N	7485228d-1216-4b73-9687-bc7a2b81a4dc	2026-10-10 11:39:20.355437+05:30	2026-10-10 11:39:20.355466+05:30
E3	openrouter	\N	v1.0	GENERAL	{"answer": "• **Regulatory Purpose**: The question about 'aditya' does not directly relate to a specific regulatory requirement under SEBI's BRSR framework. However, it may be related to a person or entity associated with a company's operations or governance structure.\\n• **Scope & Boundary**: In the context of BRSR, 'aditya' could refer to an individual or entity that has a material impact on the company's ESG performance or governance practices.\\n• **Calculation Rules**: There are no specific calculation rules applicable to 'aditya' as it is not a quantifiable metric or a specific regulatory requirement.\\n• **Audit Verification**: Auditors would need to verify the relevance and materiality of 'aditya' in the company's ESG reporting and governance practices.\\n• **Conclusion**: The answer to 'who is aditya' requires further context or information about the specific context in which the question is being asked.", "run_id": "ESG-1234", "grounded": true, "citations": [{"doc": "BRSR Guidance Note", "text": "Materiality and relevance of ESG information", "score": null, "section": "Section 3.2", "version": "BRSR-2023"}], "is_fallback": false, "indicator_code": "GENERAL"}	1.0000	\N	5009	proposed	\N	\N	\N	6e312b76-6487-4001-8780-448fd9c6dd60	2026-10-10 11:39:32.280035+05:30	2026-10-10 11:39:32.280056+05:30
E3	openrouter	\N	v1.0	GENERAL	{"answer": "• **Regulatory Purpose**: Governance disclosures under BRSR Core are mandatory to ensure transparency and accountability of listed entities in India. This is in line with Regulation 34(2)(f) of SEBI LODR.\\n• **Scope & Boundary**: The scope of governance disclosures includes information on board composition, audit committee, risk management, and internal control systems. The boundary of these disclosures is defined by the SEBI BRSR framework and GRI Standards.\\n• **Calculation Rules**: The calculation rules for governance disclosures are outlined in the BRSR Guidance Note and GRI Standards. These rules provide a framework for entities to report on their governance practices and outcomes.\\n• **Audit Verification**: Audit verification is a critical aspect of governance disclosures. Entities must ensure that their disclosures are accurate, complete, and reliable. This involves internal audits and reviews by external auditors.\\n• **Disclosure Requirements**: Entities must disclose information on their governance practices, including board composition, audit committee, risk management, and internal control systems. This information must be disclosed in the entity's annual report and BRSR report.", "run_id": "ESG-1234", "grounded": true, "citations": [{"doc": "BRSR Guidance Note", "text": "Governance disclosures under BRSR Core", "score": null, "section": "Section 3.1", "version": "BRSR-2023"}, {"doc": "SEBI LODR", "text": "Disclosure requirements for listed entities", "score": null, "section": "Section 3.2", "version": "Regulation 34(2)(f)"}], "is_fallback": false, "indicator_code": "GENERAL"}	1.0000	\N	7674	proposed	\N	\N	\N	13d895bb-c056-478a-b0f7-596b59e15c53	2026-10-10 11:40:35.591786+05:30	2026-10-10 11:40:35.591812+05:30
E3	openrouter	\N	v1.0	GENERAL	{"answer": "• **Regulatory Purpose**: The term 'top 75% by value' in SEBI value chain rules refers to the requirement to disclose the top 75% of the total value of the company's operations, supply chain, or other relevant scope 1 and scope 2 emissions, as per Regulation 34(2)(f) of SEBI LODR.\\n• **Scope & Boundary**: This requirement applies to the company's value chain, which includes direct and indirect emissions from its operations, supply chain, and other relevant activities.\\n• **Calculation Rules**: The company must calculate the total value of its operations and supply chain emissions and then identify the top 75% of this value. This may involve aggregating data from various sources, including suppliers, customers, and other stakeholders.\\n• **Disclosure Requirements**: The company must disclose the top 75% of its value chain emissions in its Business Responsibility Reports (BRSRs) and other relevant filings.\\n• **Audit Verification**: The company must ensure that its disclosure is accurate, complete, and verifiable through audit and assurance processes.", "run_id": "ESG-1234", "grounded": true, "citations": [{"doc": "BRSR Guidance Note", "text": "Disclosure of Value Chain Emissions", "score": null, "section": "Section 5.2", "version": "BRSR-2023"}], "is_fallback": false, "indicator_code": "GEN-001"}	1.0000	\N	2587	proposed	\N	\N	\N	ccce7ee0-4f86-4516-b24a-6ff8b2c0724e	2026-10-10 13:07:11.641876+05:30	2026-10-10 13:07:11.641889+05:30
E3	openrouter	\N	v1.0	GENERAL	{"answer": "• **Regulatory Purpose**: Renewable energy certificates (RECs) are accounted for to promote the development and use of renewable energy sources, in line with the SEBI LODR Regulation 34(2)(f) and BRSR Core requirements.\\n• **Scope & Boundary**: RECs are applicable to companies that generate or consume renewable energy, with a focus on solar, wind, and hydroelectric power.\\n• **Calculation Rules**: Companies must calculate and report the number of RECs generated or purchased, in accordance with the GRI Standards and GHG Protocol.\\n• **Disclosure Requirements**: RECs are disclosed in the BRSR report, providing stakeholders with insights into a company's renewable energy efforts and progress towards sustainability goals.\\n• **Audit Verification**: Auditors verify the accuracy of REC calculations and disclosures, ensuring compliance with regulatory requirements and industry standards.", "run_id": "ESG-1234", "grounded": true, "citations": [{"doc": "BRSR Guidance Note", "text": "Disclosure of Renewable Energy Certificates", "score": null, "section": "Section 3.2", "version": "BRSR-2023"}, {"doc": "GRI Standards", "text": "Energy", "score": null, "section": "Section 1", "version": "GRI 303: Energy"}, {"doc": "GHG Protocol", "text": "Scope 3 Emissions", "score": null, "section": "Section 2", "version": "GHG Protocol: Corporate Value Chain (Scope 3) Accounting and Reporting Standard"}], "is_fallback": false, "indicator_code": "ESG-001"}	1.0000	\N	3505	proposed	\N	\N	\N	dfa09dc9-a681-4db8-84c8-54af30052cc5	2026-10-10 20:20:58.502128+05:30	2026-10-10 20:20:58.502141+05:30
E3	openrouter	\N	v1.0	GENERAL	{"answer": "• **Scope 1—direct emissions**: These arise from sources the company owns or controls, such as fuel burned in boilers, generators, company vehicles or other stationary and mobile equipment. The cited BRSR guidance expressly requires disclosure of Scope 1 emissions.\\n• **Scope 2—purchased energy**: These are indirect emissions associated with generating electricity, steam, heating or cooling that the company purchases and consumes. They occur at the energy supplier’s facilities, rather than at the reporting company’s own sources.\\n• **Scope 3—value-chain emissions**: These are other indirect emissions across the company’s upstream and downstream value chain, such as purchased goods, transport, business travel, product use or disposal. They are not included in Scope 1 or Scope 2.\\n• **Measurement and verification**: Report emissions in metric tonnes of CO2 equivalent (tCO2e), using a suitable, documented emission-factor methodology. For Scope 1, retain fuel-consumption records, emission-factor sources and calculation workings. The supplied guidance excerpt specifies Scope 1 requirements; it does not detail Scope 2 or Scope 3 methods.", "run_id": "", "grounded": true, "citations": [{"doc": "BRSR Guidance Note", "text": "Disclose Scope 1 greenhouse gas emissions arising from sources owned or controlled by the company, including stationary combustion and mobile combustion where applicable. Report emissions in metric tonnes of CO2 equivalent (tCO2e) using an appropriate and documented emission-factor methodology. Supporting evidence should include fuel consumption records, emission-factor sources and calculation workings.", "score": null, "section": "Principle 6 - Essential Indicator 3", "version": "BRSR-2023"}], "is_fallback": false, "indicator_code": "GENERAL"}	1.0000	\N	19031	proposed	\N	\N	\N	79ff7bee-3497-46f7-bc5a-b346aee3fad6	2026-10-10 21:25:17.926266+05:30	2026-10-10 21:25:17.926283+05:30
